<figure class="decision-figure" id="investment-feedback" aria-labelledby="investment-feedback-title">
  <h3 id="investment-feedback-title">When is an interface check worth maintaining?</h3>
  <div class="paths" role="list" aria-label="From customer requests to the next investment">
    <div class="station" role="listitem" style="grid-row: 1">
      <div class="step"><strong>Customer requests</strong>How often would a quicker update help someone using the product?</div>
    </div>
    <div class="station" role="listitem" style="grid-row: 2">
      <div class="step"><strong>Funding decision</strong>The owner compares the cost of another manual review with the cost of building and maintaining a check.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="station" role="listitem" style="grid-row: 3">
      <div class="step"><strong>Maintained tools</strong>If funded, the interface owner keeps the unit definitions, comparison tool and reference cases current.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="station" role="listitem" style="grid-row: 4">
      <div class="step"><strong>Fast feedback on one task</strong>An agent compares the units, corrects a mismatch in its proposal and reruns the check.</div>
      <div class="returns">
        <div><strong>Back to the tools</strong>The agent can propose a better check. The interface owner reviews it against the agreed units.</div>
        <div><strong>Back to the funding decision</strong>Catching a mismatch before assembly can save rework or a second rig visit.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="station" role="listitem" style="grid-row: 5">
      <div class="step"><strong>Whole-product review</strong>Engineers integrate the change and test the receiver under load. After an authorized release, operations can put it into service.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="station last" role="listitem" style="grid-row: 6">
      <div class="step"><strong>Customer outcome in use</strong>A customer paid for this update. Did the reading reach the operator in time to act?</div>
      <div class="returns">
        <div><strong>Back to the funding decision</strong>What customers actually gained from the update informs the owner’s next investment.</div>
        <div class="possible"><strong>Possible return to customer requests</strong>A customer who benefited may ask for the same turnaround on the next order.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <span class="rail demand" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="rail funding" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="rail tools" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="saving" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  </div>
  <figcaption>Follow one change down the figure. The return paths show what could justify a better tool or another investment across successive changes. The dashed path is a hypothesis about future demand.</figcaption>
</figure>

